>
  <p>
    A family new to Allen looks for a dentist the same way it looks for everything else: on the map, on a phone. It
    checks who takes its insurance, reads the newest reviews and books with the office that makes it easy. A parent in
    Southlake researching braces does the same, only slower. More of them now ask ChatGPT or read Google’s AI answer
    first.
  </p>
  <p>
    Owner-led practices compete with groups that have marketing teams. A good independent practice can still win the
    search in its own town. Here is where most lose patients, and what fixes it.
  </p>

  <h2>Where dental practices lose patients</h2>
  <h3>A profile that does not say what you do</h3>
  <p>
    “Dentist” or “Orthodontist” as the primary category, then the secondary categories that match your real work:
    cosmetic dentist, pediatric dentist, emergency dental service, dental implants provider. List each service on the
    profile as well. A general listing competes for none of the searches that bring in higher-value work.
  </p>
  <h3>One page for every service</h3>
  <p>
    Patients search “dental implants Plano”, “Invisalign Frisco”, “emergency dentist McKinney”. A single services list
    ranks for none of those. Each core service needs its own page: what it is, who it suits, what the visit involves,
    how cost and insurance work, and the towns you serve.
  </p>
  <h3>Insurance answered too late</h3>
  <p>
    If a patient has to call to find out whether you take her plan, she will often call someone else first. Put the
    plans you accept on the site, keep the list current, and say how you handle patients who are out of network.
  </p>
  <h3>Reviews that stopped</h3>
  <p>
    Patients read the newest reviews first. A long history of reviews that ended last year loses to a steady stream
    from this season. Ask at checkout with a direct link, and reply to every review.
  </p>
  <h3>Booking only by phone</h3>
  <p>
    Many patients look in the evening, when the front desk is closed. A booking or appointment-request link on the
    profile and on every service page catches them while they are still deciding.
  </p>
  <h3>Nothing for AI to quote</h3>
  <p>
    AI assistants recommend practices they can confirm across sources. Clear service pages, consistent listings,
    current insurance information and recent, specific reviews are what they read. Our guide to
    <a href="/visibility/ai-search/">AI search</a> covers it in full.
  </p>

  <h2>Dental rules to respect</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Keep patient details out of review replies. Thank them; do not confirm they are a patient.</li>
    <li>Use only your own before-and-after photos, with the patient’s signed authorization on file.</li>
    <li>Do not promise results. Describe the treatment and who it suits.</li>
    <li>Only advertise as a specialist if you meet the Texas dental board’s specialty advertising rule. General dentists can list the services they perform without the word.</li>
    <li>Never offer discounts or gifts in exchange for reviews. Google’s review policy forbids it, and federal rules bar paying for positive ones.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    This is general guidance, not legal advice. Your licensing board’s rules and your own counsel decide what you can
    publish.
  </p>

